 ("h2", "1. Money as a Medium of Exchange"),
 ("p", "A person holding money can easily exchange it for any commodity or service. Everyone therefore prefers to receive payment in money and then exchange it for the things they want. Take a shoe manufacturer who wants to sell shoes and buy wheat. In a **barter system**, where goods are directly exchanged without money, he must find a wheat grower who also wants shoes. This is called the **double coincidence of wants**: what one person desires to sell is exactly what the other wishes to buy."),
 ("p", "In a money economy, money provides the crucial intermediate step and eliminates the need for double coincidence. The shoe manufacturer only needs to find a buyer for his shoes, and then can purchase wheat or any other commodity. Since money acts as an intermediary it is called a **medium of exchange**."),
