 ("p", "Before coins, a variety of objects were used as money; from very early ages Indians used grains and cattle. Then came metallic coins of gold, silver and copper, which continued well into the last century (punch-marked coins, Gupta coins, the gold mohar of Akbar's reign, Tughlaq coins)."),
 ("h3", "Currency"),
 ("p", "Modern forms of money include currency, that is, paper notes and coins. Modern currency is not made of precious metal and has no use of its own, unlike grain and cattle. It is accepted as a medium of exchange because it is **authorised by the government**. In India the **Reserve Bank of India** issues currency notes on behalf of the central government; no other individual or organisation is allowed to issue currency. The law legalises the rupee as a medium of payment that cannot be refused in settling transactions in India."),
 ("h3", "Deposits with banks"),
